00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:17.329
I was a developer relations
engineer on the Firebase team

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:18.022
at Google.

00:04:18.022 --> 00:04:18.980
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:04:18.980 --> 00:04:20.521
So the reason why
we have both of you

00:04:20.521 --> 00:04:24.650
here today is because today
we are releasing Cloud AutoML

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:27.260
Vision, and we wanted you both
to help share a little bit more

00:04:27.260 --> 00:04:28.260
about what that's about.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:29.664
So can you tell us what that is?

00:04:29.664 --> 00:04:32.330
SARA ROBINSON: First I will talk
a little bit about the products

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:33.746
that we currently
have if you want

00:04:33.746 --> 00:04:36.740
to do image recognition
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:04:36.740 --> 00:04:39.740
So right now your options are
to use the Vision API, which

00:04:39.740 --> 00:04:41.540
is a pre-trained
model, that gives you

00:04:41.540 --> 00:04:44.760
access to this model with
just one REST API request.

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:47.070
So you can send your
image to the API.

00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:49.040
You get back a bunch
of data on what it

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:50.202
thinks it's a picture of.

00:04:50.202 --> 00:04:51.910
If you want to do
something more custom--

00:04:51.910 --> 00:04:53.284
so the Vision API
doesn't require

00:04:53.284 --> 00:04:55.610
any training data of your own.

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:59.030
If you have a problem that's
more specific to your use case,

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:01.640
you can build and train a
custom model with TensorFlow

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:05.340
and use your own training data
to train and tune the model.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:07.820
But there's kind of been a
gap in our current product

00:05:07.820 --> 00:05:10.340
offerings, and that's why we're
really excited about Cloud

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:11.430
AutoML Vision.

00:05:11.430 --> 00:05:13.790
So what Cloud AutoML
Vision lets you do

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:17.330
is it lets developers that don't
have too much machine learning

00:05:17.330 --> 00:05:20.090
expertise to build
high quality models.

00:05:20.090 --> 00:05:23.360
So the way it works is
you upload your images--

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:25.910
they can be either a labeled
or unlabeled data set--

00:05:25.910 --> 00:05:28.690
to the Cloud AutoML Vision UI.

00:05:28.690 --> 00:05:30.690
You just press a button
to train your model.

00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:32.630
And then you can
access your model

00:05:32.630 --> 00:05:35.390
with an easy-to-use REST API.

00:05:35.390 --> 00:05:38.020
AMY UNRUH: So under the
hood, it's using both

00:05:38.020 --> 00:05:40.100
state-of-the-art
transfer learning,

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:43.010
and something called
neural architecture search.

00:05:43.010 --> 00:05:46.430
Transfer learning-- essentially
what that is, is you

00:05:46.430 --> 00:05:48.620
leverage an already
trained model.

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:50.210
It's already trained
[? weights. ?]

00:05:50.210 --> 00:05:54.290
And it turns out that the
so-called embeddings that

00:05:54.290 --> 00:05:56.840
are learned, as part of the
process of training this model,

00:05:56.840 --> 00:06:00.320
incorporate really useful
abstractions related

00:06:00.320 --> 00:06:03.110
to image classification
that transfer

00:06:03.110 --> 00:06:05.990
to things that the original
model wasn't actually

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:06.710
trained on.

00:06:06.710 --> 00:06:10.190
So you can essentially take an
already trained model and tap

00:06:10.190 --> 00:06:12.670
into it at the so-called
bottleneck layer--

00:06:12.670 --> 00:06:16.610
a layer of embedding just
before the output of the model--

00:06:16.610 --> 00:06:21.020
and use that to bootstrap
training of new image

00:06:21.020 --> 00:06:21.990
classifications.

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:24.680
So you can take a
neural net that's

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:27.680
trained on a bunch of things
like different animals

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:29.870
and boats and cars
and things like that,

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:32.300
and you can train
it on a new data

00:06:32.300 --> 00:06:35.270
set, your own data,
new classifications,

00:06:35.270 --> 00:06:38.850
very successfully, without
needing very much data.

00:06:38.850 --> 00:06:40.430
So that's transfer learning.

00:06:40.430 --> 00:06:44.120
MELANIE WARRICK: So with our
previous Cloud Vision API,

00:06:44.120 --> 00:06:46.260
people were able to
use it for problems,

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:49.400
but a lot of times
their image analysis.

00:06:49.400 --> 00:06:52.890
But they were limited to what's
provided in that Cloud Vision

00:06:52.890 --> 00:06:53.390
API?

00:06:53.390 --> 00:06:56.210
There wasn't as much flexibility
for their specific data sets.

00:06:56.210 --> 00:06:57.530
Is that what I'm understanding?

00:06:57.530 --> 00:06:58.821
AMY UNRUH: Yeah that's correct.

00:06:58.821 --> 00:07:02.360
So the Vision API has a
fixed set of categories

00:07:02.360 --> 00:07:03.250
that it recognizes.

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:04.940
A very large number
of categories,

00:07:04.940 --> 00:07:07.360
but they're still
fixed categories.

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:09.260
So if you're in a
specialized domain,

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:12.470
it's not going to distinguish
your own domain-specific

00:07:12.470 --> 00:07:14.427
categories the way that
you would like it to.

00:07:14.427 --> 00:07:16.760
MARK MANDEL: I was going to
ask, what sort of categories

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:18.460
would you be talking about
then, that you think are,

00:07:18.460 --> 00:07:19.430
quote unquote, "specialized"?

00:07:19.430 --> 00:07:20.460
What does that mean?

00:07:20.460 --> 00:07:22.410
AMY UNRUH: Well, one
really good example,

00:07:22.410 --> 00:07:26.930
one of the early customers
is using AutoML Vision

00:07:26.930 --> 00:07:29.120
to try to do
specialized training

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:31.860
of large number of different
wildlife categories.

00:07:31.860 --> 00:07:34.730
So this is something
that AutoML Vision is not

00:07:34.730 --> 00:07:38.296
going to be able to detect with
the right level of specificity.

00:07:38.296 --> 00:07:39.920
SARA ROBINSON: Another
possible example

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:42.860
is, let's say you work in
the manufacturing industry,

00:07:42.860 --> 00:07:45.380
and you've got very
specific parts that you're

00:07:45.380 --> 00:07:47.330
using on some sort
of assembly line,

00:07:47.330 --> 00:07:49.460
and you want to
determine whether there's

00:07:49.460 --> 00:07:51.670
a faulty part in some
sort of the process.

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:54.930
So likely you have a
lot of training data,

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:58.130
images of different parts
of this assembly line.

00:07:58.130 --> 00:08:01.970
You could train a custom model
to identify a broken part.

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:04.430
MELANIE WARRICK: And that's
with the AutoML Vision that's

00:08:04.430 --> 00:08:05.770
been released as of today.

00:08:05.770 --> 00:08:08.270
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, so when
we would speak about the Vision

00:08:08.270 --> 00:08:11.180
API before, the first
question we would always get

00:08:11.180 --> 00:08:13.490
is, how can I make
this more customized?

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:15.690
Until today, we didn't
have an answer for that.

00:08:15.690 --> 00:08:18.410
So that's why we're super
excited about AutoML Vision.

00:08:18.410 --> 00:08:19.520
MARK MANDEL: So Sara,
you said something

00:08:19.520 --> 00:08:22.061
a little earlier that I actually
just wanted to come back to.

00:08:22.061 --> 00:08:24.090
You said that
people could provide

00:08:24.090 --> 00:08:26.420
both labeled and
unlabeled images.

00:08:26.420 --> 00:08:29.160
Like, I can get what
happens with labeled images.

00:08:29.160 --> 00:08:32.632
But if I hand somebody unlabeled
images, how does that work?

00:08:32.632 --> 00:08:34.340
Doesn't somebody have
to provide the data

00:08:34.340 --> 00:08:35.539
about what something is?

00:08:35.539 --> 00:08:37.372
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah,
so this is pretty cool.

00:08:37.372 --> 00:08:39.169
One feature of AutoML
Vision is that you

00:08:39.169 --> 00:08:42.320
can have a team of
in-house human labelers

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:44.450
that will review
your instructions,

00:08:44.450 --> 00:08:46.940
and they'll classify
your images for you.

00:08:46.940 --> 00:08:49.340
So often people have already
labeled their data sets,

00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:51.230
but there's some cases
where you might just

00:08:51.230 --> 00:08:53.420
have a bunch of images
that are unlabeled.

00:08:53.420 --> 00:08:56.940
So the way this works is you
provide a few instructions.

00:08:56.940 --> 00:09:00.590
So for the image categories
that you're hoping the labelers

00:09:00.590 --> 00:09:03.410
will use to label your
images, you can provide a few,

00:09:03.410 --> 00:09:06.200
what they call,
exemplar images, to say,

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:09.260
this is a really good example of
whatever specific label you're

00:09:09.260 --> 00:09:12.170
trying to provide,
and the human labelers

00:09:12.170 --> 00:09:14.980
will work on labeling
your images accordingly.

00:09:14.980 --> 00:09:16.730
Then you'll get a
set of labeled images

00:09:16.730 --> 00:09:19.344
back that you can feed into
AutoML Vision for training.

00:09:19.344 --> 00:09:21.260
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh, so
there's human labelers

00:09:21.260 --> 00:09:22.250
who can help with this.

00:09:22.250 --> 00:09:24.920
AMY UNRUH: And AutoML
Vision also has a nice UI.

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:27.080
It'll detect-- if
you give a data set,

00:09:27.080 --> 00:09:29.420
not all of the images
in the data set

00:09:29.420 --> 00:09:31.490
have to be already
labeled, and it'll

00:09:31.490 --> 00:09:34.640
identify the ones for you
that aren't labeled, and has

00:09:34.640 --> 00:09:38.030
a nice UI that lets you label
them interactively, if there

00:09:38.030 --> 00:09:39.860
aren't a huge number of them.

00:09:39.860 --> 00:09:40.790
MELANIE WARRICK: Kind
of touching further

00:09:40.790 --> 00:09:42.680
on this labeling-- we were
talking a little bit about this

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:43.340
before.

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:46.460
How is this different
between AutoML Vision

00:09:46.460 --> 00:09:49.970
and the original Cloud
Vision API for labeling?

00:09:49.970 --> 00:09:53.930
AMY UNRUH: Cloud Vision API only
exposes the prediction aspect.

00:09:53.930 --> 00:09:56.450
It doesn't let you provide
your own labels at all.

00:09:56.450 --> 00:09:59.730
You give it images, and it
tells you the classifications

00:09:59.730 --> 00:10:00.650
that it's detected.

00:10:00.650 --> 00:10:04.396
But you cannot tune it with your
own images and your own labels.

00:10:04.396 --> 00:10:06.770
MARK MANDEL: I was just curious
as well for the developer

00:10:06.770 --> 00:10:07.520
point of view.

00:10:07.520 --> 00:10:08.360
How does that look?

00:10:08.360 --> 00:10:11.000
Is there like a special API
I need to push my images to?

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:13.020
Do I push it to, like,
Google Cloud Storage?

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:14.450
What's that flow look like?

00:10:14.450 --> 00:10:15.500
SARA ROBINSON: Are you
talking about generating

00:10:15.500 --> 00:10:16.199
the predictions?

00:10:16.199 --> 00:10:18.740
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so like if
I want to provide my own images

00:10:18.740 --> 00:10:20.564
and do my own labels,
how do I get that in?

00:10:20.564 --> 00:10:22.980
SARA ROBINSON: There's a couple
different ways to do that.

00:10:22.980 --> 00:10:25.580
So first, if you want to try out
your model right away once it's

00:10:25.580 --> 00:10:27.330
been trained, you can
actually just upload

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:29.221
images for prediction
in the UI, and see

00:10:29.221 --> 00:10:30.470
what predictions you get back.

00:10:30.470 --> 00:10:32.011
But then obviously
after you do that,

00:10:32.011 --> 00:10:34.070
you'll want to build an
app that probably queries

00:10:34.070 --> 00:10:35.580
your newly trained model.

00:10:35.580 --> 00:10:39.680
And to do that, you can
either use the Cloud ML Engine

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:43.170
prediction API or you can
use the Cloud Vision API.

00:10:43.170 --> 00:10:45.670
So if you're already using
the Vision API in production,

00:10:45.670 --> 00:10:46.760
and let's say you
want to switch.

00:10:46.760 --> 00:10:48.260
You have a lot of
training data, you

00:10:48.260 --> 00:10:50.180
want to train a model on AutoML.

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:53.150
You actually just have to make
a few small updates to the way

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:54.860
your Vision API request looks.

00:10:54.860 --> 00:10:56.360
So instead of label
detection, you

00:10:56.360 --> 00:10:58.520
would use custom
model detection.

00:10:58.520 --> 00:11:00.295
And then you pass it
the ID of the model

00:11:00.295 --> 00:11:01.670
that you just
trained, and you're

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:04.190
good to go generating
predictions with the Vision

00:11:04.190 --> 00:11:04.740
API.

00:11:04.740 --> 00:11:05.130
MARK MANDEL: Sweet.

00:11:05.130 --> 00:11:07.640
And if I have a whole bunch
of data I want to upload,

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:09.300
I'm guessing I'm not going
to do that through the UI.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:11.260
There's some sort of
automated pipeline for that?

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:12.210
SARA ROBINSON: For training?

00:11:12.210 --> 00:11:12.710
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:11:12.710 --> 00:11:13.543
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah.

00:11:13.543 --> 00:11:15.560
So you can either
upload images locally

00:11:15.560 --> 00:11:18.590
from your computer or you
can put all of your images

00:11:18.590 --> 00:11:21.950
in Google Cloud Storage
and then create a CSV file

00:11:21.950 --> 00:11:25.334
with the URL of your image
and the associated label,

00:11:25.334 --> 00:11:26.000
if you have one.

00:11:26.000 --> 00:11:27.620
MELANIE WARRICK: And if I
have any new images after I've

00:11:27.620 --> 00:11:30.590
got a trained model, what
would that look like for this?

00:11:30.590 --> 00:11:33.170
AMY UNRUH: You could
iteratively add new images

00:11:33.170 --> 00:11:34.340
to your data set.

00:11:34.340 --> 00:11:35.180
So that works well.

00:11:35.180 --> 00:11:36.980
And then you can just
retrain once you've

00:11:36.980 --> 00:11:38.210
added the new images.

00:11:38.210 --> 00:11:39.200
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:11:39.200 --> 00:11:41.533
And then you were saying it's
a graphical user interface

00:11:41.533 --> 00:11:44.630
that's can be provided
basically on the GCP website

00:11:44.630 --> 00:11:47.240
in the same areas they
can find the other APIs?

00:11:47.240 --> 00:11:49.820
AMY UNRUH: Currently it's
a little more standalone

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:50.757
in terms of the URL.

00:11:50.757 --> 00:11:51.590
MELANIE WARRICK: OK.

00:11:51.590 --> 00:11:53.360
AMY UNRUH: But
it's a very nice UI

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:56.360
that lets you inspect
valuation results,

00:11:56.360 --> 00:11:58.435
run online predictions,
the whole deal.

00:11:58.435 --> 00:11:59.810
SARA ROBINSON:
Eventually it will

00:11:59.810 --> 00:12:03.320
be part of the cloud console,
but the alpha version,

00:12:03.320 --> 00:12:05.540
as Amy mentioned,
is a separate UI.

00:12:05.540 --> 00:12:06.614
MARK MANDEL: OK, cool.

00:12:06.614 --> 00:12:08.530
Sara, you said something
else in passing, too,

00:12:08.530 --> 00:12:10.790
and I think it's probably
worth going a little deeper on,

00:12:10.790 --> 00:12:12.740
where you're talking about once
you've got a trained model that

00:12:12.740 --> 00:12:14.480
works in Cloud
Vision API, you're

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:16.230
able to access it
through the Cloud Vision

00:12:16.230 --> 00:12:17.460
API, which is really cool.

00:12:17.460 --> 00:12:18.876
But the second
thing you said was,

00:12:18.876 --> 00:12:21.787
you're also able to export it
through the Cloud ML Engine.

00:12:21.787 --> 00:12:23.870
Can you just talk a bit
about what that product is

00:12:23.870 --> 00:12:25.744
and how that would work
for people who aren't

00:12:25.744 --> 00:12:27.180
familiar with Cloud ML Engine?

00:12:27.180 --> 00:12:28.263
SARA ROBINSON: Sure, yeah.

00:12:28.263 --> 00:12:29.960
Cloud ML Engine is
a product that we

00:12:29.960 --> 00:12:33.900
provide that lets you build your
own custom TensorFlow model.

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:37.250
And then you can use ML
Engine to run your model

00:12:37.250 --> 00:12:39.140
training in the cloud,
and also to deploy

00:12:39.140 --> 00:12:40.610
your model for serving.

00:12:40.610 --> 00:12:44.730
And this is actually what AutoML
Vision is using under the hood.

00:12:44.730 --> 00:12:46.960
So if you're already
using Cloud ML Engine

00:12:46.960 --> 00:12:49.850
and you're more comfortable
with that API for generating

00:12:49.850 --> 00:12:51.860
predictions, this
is just another way

00:12:51.860 --> 00:12:54.275
that you can generate
predictions with AutoML.

00:12:54.275 --> 00:12:55.400
MELANIE WARRICK: All right.

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:58.024
Amy, you were touching before on
the neural architecture search

00:12:58.024 --> 00:12:58.670
technology.

00:12:58.670 --> 00:13:01.430
There was some blog post
that was released in November

00:13:01.430 --> 00:13:04.850
around some of the work that was
done, analysis to scale that.

00:13:04.850 --> 00:13:07.250
Can you tell us a little
bit more about what that is

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:08.410
and how that's used?

00:13:08.410 --> 00:13:10.940
AMY UNRUH: Yeah, so we'll
point to some links in the show

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:11.620
notes.

00:13:11.620 --> 00:13:14.720
What's typically meant by
neural architecture search

00:13:14.720 --> 00:13:18.050
is performing some type
of neural net-based search

00:13:18.050 --> 00:13:20.060
of neural net
architectures that are

00:13:20.060 --> 00:13:21.739
useful for a particular task.

00:13:21.739 --> 00:13:24.030
And there are different
variants of this-- for example,

00:13:24.030 --> 00:13:25.760
there's an
evolutionary approach,

00:13:25.760 --> 00:13:29.150
and there is a reinforcement
learning-based approach.

00:13:29.150 --> 00:13:32.750
In one type of neural
architecture search framework,

00:13:32.750 --> 00:13:35.090
there is something
called a controller

00:13:35.090 --> 00:13:36.080
part of the framework.

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:40.520
It's a recurrent neural
net and what it does is it

00:13:40.520 --> 00:13:44.000
samples child networks with
different architectures--

00:13:44.000 --> 00:13:45.590
tries out different
architectures.

00:13:45.590 --> 00:13:48.770
And then the accuracy
of a given child network

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:51.590
is used to update the
controller for its next round

00:13:51.590 --> 00:13:53.270
of architecture generation.

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:57.470
One key insight is that
for image classification

00:13:57.470 --> 00:14:01.040
problems-- for example you often
use convolutional neural nets.

00:14:01.040 --> 00:14:05.480
They often have repeated
motifs, like for example,

00:14:05.480 --> 00:14:08.990
different combinations of
convolutional filter banks.

00:14:08.990 --> 00:14:11.300
And so sort of a
key observation then

00:14:11.300 --> 00:14:15.110
is that you can build
interesting new architectures

00:14:15.110 --> 00:14:17.480
in your search space
of new architectures

00:14:17.480 --> 00:14:20.540
by stacking up these motifs,
these different pieces

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:21.800
of the neural net.

00:14:21.800 --> 00:14:24.800
So that's technology behind
what we're using here.

00:14:24.800 --> 00:14:27.134
AutoML is leveraging
the results of some

00:14:27.134 --> 00:14:28.550
of this neural
architecture search

00:14:28.550 --> 00:14:30.950
technology, and in
particular, kind

00:14:30.950 --> 00:14:33.590
of leveraging this ability
to explore the search

00:14:33.590 --> 00:14:36.360
space by kind of stacking
up generic cells.

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:37.700
So it's very interesting stuff.

00:14:37.700 --> 00:14:39.784
And again, we'll put some
links in the show notes.

00:14:39.784 --> 00:14:41.200
MELANIE WARRICK:
And that's great.

00:14:41.200 --> 00:14:43.610
And my understanding too
is that the nice thing

00:14:43.610 --> 00:14:45.710
is that this type of
approach is allowing

00:14:45.710 --> 00:14:48.600
us to then do AutoML, not
require the people who

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:52.160
are using the tool to
really have a PhD in ML,

00:14:52.160 --> 00:14:55.640
to have to know and be an expert
in terms of building models

00:14:55.640 --> 00:14:57.290
and tuning models.

00:14:57.290 --> 00:14:58.496
AMY UNRUH: Exactly, yeah.

00:14:58.496 --> 00:15:00.620
MELANIE WARRICK: That's
the whole intent behind it.

00:15:00.620 --> 00:15:02.369
AMY UNRUH: And in
fact, perhaps we

00:15:02.369 --> 00:15:04.660
should have mentioned this
earlier when we were talking

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:05.890
about transfer learning.

00:15:05.890 --> 00:15:08.560
Those who are a little more
familiar with neural nets

00:15:08.560 --> 00:15:11.440
might have tried, for
example, to do transfer

00:15:11.440 --> 00:15:13.510
any examples using TensorFlow.

00:15:13.510 --> 00:15:15.130
And so if you've
tried that, you'll

00:15:15.130 --> 00:15:17.065
know that it takes a
little bit of coding

00:15:17.065 --> 00:15:19.330
and you have to kind
of figure out how

00:15:19.330 --> 00:15:21.020
to plug things into each other.

00:15:21.020 --> 00:15:23.410
So with AutoML Vision,
there's none of that.

00:15:23.410 --> 00:15:25.870
You don't need to do any
coding yourself-- or figure out

00:15:25.870 --> 00:15:26.590
any of that.

00:15:26.590 --> 00:15:29.200
But you're leveraging
state-of-the-art approach kind

00:15:29.200 --> 00:15:30.160
of in the same manner.

00:15:30.160 --> 00:15:31.118
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:15:31.118 --> 00:15:33.460
In terms of AutoML, we've
been talking about it

00:15:33.460 --> 00:15:34.900
as Cloud AutoML Vision.

00:15:34.900 --> 00:15:36.760
It's focused on vision.

00:15:36.760 --> 00:15:40.210
Is there intent, or what's
the future look like for this?

00:15:40.210 --> 00:15:42.950
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, so this
is just the first of the AutoML

00:15:42.950 --> 00:15:43.450
products.

00:15:43.450 --> 00:15:46.372
We can't talk in too much
detail about what's coming next.

00:15:46.372 --> 00:15:47.080
MARK MANDEL: Aww.

00:15:47.080 --> 00:15:47.455
SARA ROBINSON: I know.

00:15:47.455 --> 00:15:48.286
It's a bummer.

00:15:48.286 --> 00:15:49.660
There are teams
that are actively

00:15:49.660 --> 00:15:53.800
working on other products
under this AutoML umbrella

00:15:53.800 --> 00:15:56.470
to accomplish other common
machine learning tasks

00:15:56.470 --> 00:15:58.764
like we already provide
in our Cloud ML APIs.

00:15:58.764 --> 00:16:00.180
MELANIE WARRICK:
So more's coming.

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:03.910
I mean, this tool in particular
is playing into this whole AI

00:16:03.910 --> 00:16:06.220
democratizing MLs,
trying to make

00:16:06.220 --> 00:16:08.650
things easier for
people to apply ML

00:16:08.650 --> 00:16:09.942
to their products and services.

00:16:09.942 --> 00:16:11.274
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, definitely.

00:16:11.274 --> 00:16:13.520
So if you're coming from an
app developer background,

00:16:13.520 --> 00:16:16.330
and you don't have any
machine learning experience,

00:16:16.330 --> 00:16:19.600
this tool provides a really
easy-to-use UI for you

00:16:19.600 --> 00:16:21.070
to be able to train
a custom model

00:16:21.070 --> 00:16:24.040
and then just use one API
call to generate predictions

00:16:24.040 --> 00:16:24.770
against it.

00:16:24.770 --> 00:16:25.390
MELANIE WARRICK: So
part of the reason

00:16:25.390 --> 00:16:26.806
why we got the two
of you together

00:16:26.806 --> 00:16:28.480
was also because
you guys have been

00:16:28.480 --> 00:16:29.837
doing some demos around this.

00:16:29.837 --> 00:16:32.170
Did you want to speak a little
bit to what kind of demos

00:16:32.170 --> 00:16:33.994
and what it's been
like using the API?

00:16:33.994 --> 00:16:35.410
SARA ROBINSON: So,
yeah, it's been

00:16:35.410 --> 00:16:37.360
great to be able to
try out this product

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:39.370
and provide feedback
before the launch.

00:16:39.370 --> 00:16:41.950
And one demo that
I worked on is I

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:44.290
compiled a data set
of cloud photos,

00:16:44.290 --> 00:16:47.230
because I thought it would
be cool and kind of funny

00:16:47.230 --> 00:16:49.710
if I used the cloud
to recognize clouds.

00:16:49.710 --> 00:16:50.460
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:16:50.460 --> 00:16:51.960
SARA ROBINSON: So
what I didn't know

00:16:51.960 --> 00:16:54.340
before I started doing this
is that there's over 10

00:16:54.340 --> 00:16:55.910
different types of clouds.

00:16:55.910 --> 00:16:56.869
And they all--

00:16:56.869 --> 00:16:57.910
MELANIE WARRICK: Only 10?

00:16:57.910 --> 00:16:59.050
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, I
would have expected more.

00:16:59.050 --> 00:17:00.675
SARA ROBINSON: There's
definitely more.

00:17:00.675 --> 00:17:02.800
There's like 10
high-level types.

00:17:02.800 --> 00:17:04.300
MELANIE WARRICK: I
was going to say,

00:17:04.300 --> 00:17:07.540
I thought clouds
were like snowflakes.

00:17:07.540 --> 00:17:08.040
Go ahead.

00:17:08.040 --> 00:17:10.581
SARA ROBINSON: Yeah, if there's
any meteorologists listening,

00:17:10.581 --> 00:17:13.089
they'll probably say that
there's way, way more than 10.

00:17:13.089 --> 00:17:15.271
But I boiled it down
to 10 categories.

00:17:15.271 --> 00:17:17.020
And all of these
different types of clouds

00:17:17.020 --> 00:17:19.540
obviously reveal different
things about upcoming weather

00:17:19.540 --> 00:17:20.040
patterns.

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:23.302
So you can use images of clouds
to predict weather patterns.

00:17:23.302 --> 00:17:24.760
So I wanted to see
if I could build

00:17:24.760 --> 00:17:27.814
a model that was able to
detect different cloud types.

00:17:27.814 --> 00:17:29.230
While doing some
research on this,

00:17:29.230 --> 00:17:30.650
I tried out the Vision API.

00:17:30.650 --> 00:17:33.000
So I got a couple of different
types of cloud images,

00:17:33.000 --> 00:17:34.995
sent them to the
existing Vision API,

00:17:34.995 --> 00:17:37.060
and the Vision API was
able to identify them

00:17:37.060 --> 00:17:38.980
as clouds, obviously,
but it didn't really

00:17:38.980 --> 00:17:40.252
get more specific than that.

00:17:40.252 --> 00:17:41.710
Which is fine, I
wouldn't expect it

00:17:41.710 --> 00:17:44.440
to be able to recognize
different types of clouds.

00:17:44.440 --> 00:17:46.900
So what I did is I
gathered training data,

00:17:46.900 --> 00:17:49.690
I got a bunch of different
images of all of these 10

00:17:49.690 --> 00:17:54.350
types, and I used that to
train a model with AutoML,

00:17:54.350 --> 00:17:56.120
and it ended up
working very well.

00:17:56.120 --> 00:17:59.100
So now I'm able to feed an
image of a cloud to the model,

00:17:59.100 --> 00:18:01.330
and it can tell me what
type of cloud it is.

00:18:01.330 --> 00:18:05.290
And using that I can predict
what type of weather is coming.

00:18:05.290 --> 00:18:08.755
And actually to do this, I
worked with a meteorologist

00:18:08.755 --> 00:18:09.255
at Google.

00:18:09.255 --> 00:18:10.570
His name is Rob Carver.

00:18:10.570 --> 00:18:12.467
He was a huge help
in making sure

00:18:12.467 --> 00:18:14.050
that I labeled these
images correctly.

00:18:14.050 --> 00:18:15.760
Obviously I'm not
a domain expert

00:18:15.760 --> 00:18:17.770
in identifying types of clouds.

00:18:17.770 --> 00:18:21.820
So he was able to help both
in finding photos and labeling

00:18:21.820 --> 00:18:22.720
them correctly.

00:18:22.720 --> 00:18:24.280
So that was a huge help.

00:18:24.280 --> 00:18:24.640
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:25.880
And Amy, what about your demo?

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:27.421
AMY UNRUH: So I've
been experimenting

00:18:27.421 --> 00:18:32.590
with a data set of coastline
images taken by drones.

00:18:32.590 --> 00:18:35.790
And this is a data set
courtesy of Texas A&amp;M

00:18:35.790 --> 00:18:37.540
that they were nice
enough to make public.

00:18:37.540 --> 00:18:39.940
So we'll release it
in the show notes.

00:18:39.940 --> 00:18:43.060
So this is a data
set full of images

00:18:43.060 --> 00:18:46.210
labeled with all sorts
of esoteric labels,

00:18:46.210 --> 00:18:49.360
things like "exposed riprap
structures," whatever

00:18:49.360 --> 00:18:50.350
those are.

00:18:50.350 --> 00:18:51.142
"Sheltered scarps."

00:18:51.142 --> 00:18:53.099
MARK MANDEL: I don't know
what that is, either.

00:18:53.099 --> 00:18:54.970
AMY UNRUH: "Salt
brackish water marshes."

00:18:54.970 --> 00:18:56.350
Images labeled like that.

00:18:56.350 --> 00:19:00.880
And so I'm experimenting
with how well can I

00:19:00.880 --> 00:19:05.620
use AutoML to be able to
correctly label usage images.

00:19:05.620 --> 00:19:07.870
And I should add something
we haven't really

00:19:07.870 --> 00:19:10.420
talked very much about--
the AutoML UI has

00:19:10.420 --> 00:19:14.290
a really nice interface for
inspecting what kind of results

00:19:14.290 --> 00:19:15.790
you got from your
training-- things

00:19:15.790 --> 00:19:18.850
like area under the
curve, precision recall

00:19:18.850 --> 00:19:22.680
metrics, the so-called
confusion matrix that

00:19:22.680 --> 00:19:25.640
lets you see how well your
prediction did for each label.

00:19:25.640 --> 00:19:27.670
So this has been
really helpful as I've

00:19:27.670 --> 00:19:28.900
been experimenting with this.

00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:32.050
And I'm finding that some of
these different coastline types

00:19:32.050 --> 00:19:35.640
are predicted really easily,
and some not so easily.

00:19:35.640 --> 00:19:38.080
And that would be
an indication maybe

00:19:38.080 --> 00:19:40.990
of where you wanted to find more
data and retrain your model.

00:19:40.990 --> 00:19:42.600
So it's been really
fun to play with.

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:44.470
Again, we'll put in
the show notes links

00:19:44.470 --> 00:19:46.021
